body:
![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

Using array spreading '[...(groups.get(key) ?? []), event]' inside a loop creates a new array and copies all existing elements on every iteration, resulting in O(N^2) time complexity in the worst case.

Instead, retrieve the existing array, push the new event directly, and set it if it doesn't exist. This is much more efficient and avoids unnecessary memory allocations.

```suggestion
function groupEvents(events: GuiPulseWorkerActivityEvent[], keyFor: (event: GuiPulseWorkerActivityEvent) => string): Map<string, GuiPulseWorkerActivityEvent[]> {
  const groups = new Map<string, GuiPulseWorkerActivityEvent[]>();
  for (const event of events) {
    const key = keyFor(event);
    let group = groups.get(key);
    if (!group) {
      group = [];
      groups.set(key, group);
    }
    group.push(event);
  }
  return groups;
}
```

